course: dictionary
lesson: 2119
title: Conversation: talk and making conversation
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A conversation is a talk between two or more people, where they share ideas, news or feelings. Read the words around it to see which meaning fits. «I have the gift of conversation» means being very good at talking with people.
explain: In stories, a conversation is often shown with speech marks. Alice thinks, «and what is the use of a book, without pictures or conversations?» Here conversations means the characters talking to each other.
explain: «Making conversation» is a different phrase. It means talking politely to someone, often just to be friendly. Check your meaning by putting it in the sentence: if «talk» still makes sense, you have got it.
fact: A conversation is a talk between two or more people.
fact: In a conversation, people share ideas, news or feelings.
fact: «Make conversation» means to talk politely to someone, often just to be friendly.
fact: In stories, conversations are shown with speech marks, like "Hello," said Alice.
fact: A salon is a gathering of clever people for conversation.
exercise: «I have the gift of conversation.» What does this suggest? | *Being good at talking with people | A love of long walks | A box of wrapped presents | A talent for music
exercise: Two friends talk about football at lunch. What is this talk called? | A bot | A salon | *A conversation | A chrysalis
exercise: Mum says, «Make conversation with Auntie Jo, she is lonely.» What should you do? | Give her a long speech about bots | *Talk politely to her, just to be friendly | Run off to the shops | Ignore her quietly
test: «In long conversations a bot can lose track of things said much earlier.» What does conversations mean here? | *Talks between two or more speakers, such as a person and a bot | Gardens full of flowers | Music played loudly | Ways of cooking food
test: What do the words «join the conversation» mean? | Leave the room at once | *Take part in the talk | Sing a song loudly | Write a letter to a friend
exam: «I've been told I ought to have a salon, whatever that may be.» What is a salon? | *A gathering of clever people for conversation | Kind of cake | A type of bus | A large garden
